WEBVTT

00:00:00.275 --> 00:00:04.232
Subqueries can appear in different clauses because each clause expects a different role.

00:00:04.232 --> 00:00:06.692
A subquery in WHERE contributes to filtering.

00:00:06.692 --> 00:00:09.662
A scalar subquery in SELECT becomes an output value.

00:00:09.662 --> 00:00:12.232
A subquery in FROM becomes an intermediate table.

00:00:12.232 --> 00:00:16.552
Choose the placement based on what you need the inner result to do.

00:00:16.552 --> 00:00:19.057
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:19.057 --> 00:00:24.087
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:24.087 --> 00:00:26.062
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:26.062 --> 00:00:30.752
In a real setting such as a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up,

00:00:30.752 --> 00:00:36.397
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:36.397 --> 00:00:37.987
before discussing syntax.

00:00:37.987 --> 00:00:42.147
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
